  <h1>Elasticities of Demand</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Price elasticity of demand (PED), income elasticity of demand (YED), and their implications.</p>
</div>

<div class="learning-goals">
  <div class="learning-goals-title">You should be able to</div>
  <ul>
    <li>Define PED and calculate it from data</li>
    <li>Classify PED values (elastic, inelastic, unit elastic, perfectly elastic/inelastic)</li>
    <li>Explain the determinants of PED</li>
    <li>Explain the relationship between PED and total revenue</li>
    <li>Define YED and calculate it from data</li>
    <li>Classify goods using YED (normal, inferior, necessity, luxury)</li>
    <li>Explain why elasticity matters for indirect taxes</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="tabs">
  <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="switchTab(this,'notes-2-5')">Notes</button>
  <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'worked-2-5')">Worked Examples</button>
  <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'practice-2-5')">Practice</button>
</div>

<div class="tab-content active" id="notes-2-5">

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Price Elasticity of Demand (PED)</h2>
    <p>PED is a measure of the responsiveness of the quantity of a good demanded to changes in its price. If quantity demanded is highly responsive to a price change, demand is <strong>price elastic</strong>. If not very responsive, demand is <strong>price inelastic</strong>.</p>

    <div class="formula-box">
      <div class="formula-label">PED Formula</div>
      <div class="formula">PED = % change in Qd ÷ % change in P = (ΔQ/Q) ÷ (ΔP/P)</div>
    </div>

    <p>PED is always negative (price and quantity move in opposite directions). We use the absolute value when classifying elasticity.</p>

    <h3>Values of PED</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Value (absolute)</th><th>Description</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>|PED| &gt; 1</td><td>Elastic</td><td>Quantity changes proportionately more than price</td></tr>
        <tr><td>|PED| &lt; 1</td><td>Inelastic</td><td>Quantity changes proportionately less than price</td></tr>
        <tr><td>|PED| = 1</td><td>Unit elastic</td><td>Quantity changes by the same proportion as price</td></tr>
        <tr><td>|PED| = 0</td><td>Perfectly inelastic</td><td>Quantity does not change regardless of price (vertical demand curve)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>|PED| = ∞</td><td>Perfectly elastic</td><td>Any price rise results in zero demand (horizontal demand curve)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/elastic-vs-inelastic-demand.svg" alt="A steep inelastic demand curve and a flat elastic demand curve compared for the same price change" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>For the same price change, quantity barely moves on a steep (inelastic) curve but moves a lot on a flat (elastic) curve.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="graph-figure wide">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/extreme-elasticities.svg" alt="Perfectly inelastic (vertical) and perfectly elastic (horizontal) demand and supply curves" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The extreme cases: perfectly inelastic (PED/PES = 0, vertical) and perfectly elastic (= ∞, horizontal).</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>Determinants of PED</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Determinant</th><th>More elastic when…</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Availability of substitutes</strong></td><td>Many close substitutes exist — consumers can easily switch</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Necessity vs luxury</strong></td><td>Good is a luxury — consumers can choose not to buy if price rises</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Proportion of income</strong></td><td>Large proportion of income spent — price changes have a big impact on what is left to spend</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Width of definition</strong></td><td>Narrow definition — e.g. "Toyota Prius" is more elastic than "a car"</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Time</strong></td><td>Longer time to adjust — consumers find alternatives over time</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>PED and total revenue</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Elasticity</th><th>Price rises → Revenue…</th><th>Price falls → Revenue…</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Elastic (|PED| &gt; 1)</td><td>Falls (Qd falls proportionately more)</td><td>Rises</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Inelastic (|PED| &lt; 1)</td><td>Rises (Qd falls proportionately less)</td><td>Falls</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Unit elastic (|PED| = 1)</td><td>Unchanged</td><td>Unchanged</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/ped-and-total-revenue.svg" alt="Along an elastic demand curve, a fall in price produces a larger total revenue rectangle" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>On an elastic demand curve, a price fall (P&#8321;→P&#8322;) raises total revenue: TR&#8322; is much larger than TR&#8321;.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="callout hl-only">
      <div class="callout-title">HL only: PED changes along a straight demand curve</div>
      <p>A straight demand curve has a constant gradient, so ΔQ/ΔP is constant. But PED = (ΔQ/ΔP) × (P/Q), and P/Q changes along the line:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Top of the curve</strong> (high P, low Q): P/Q is large, so demand is <strong>elastic</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Midpoint:</strong> unit elastic, and total revenue is at its maximum</li>
        <li><strong>Bottom of the curve</strong> (low P, high Q): P/Q is small, so demand is <strong>inelastic</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p>So a straight line is not "elastic" or "inelastic" as a whole (except the vertical and horizontal special cases). Its slope alone doesn't tell you its elasticity.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout hl-only">
      <div class="callout-title">HL only: PED of primary vs manufactured goods</div>
      <p><strong>Primary commodities usually have inelastic demand:</strong> staple foods and fuels are necessities; there are few substitutes; they're a small share of income; and demand is derived, since raw materials are a small part of a final product's cost, so manufacturers keep buying when prices rise.</p>
      <p><strong>Manufactured goods usually have more elastic demand:</strong> many brands and substitutes; often luxuries or durables, so purchases can be postponed; a larger share of income.</p>
      <p><strong>So what?</strong> Inelastic demand plus inelastic supply (see <a href="/ibecon/unit2/2-6/">2.6</a>) makes primary commodity prices very volatile: a small shift in either curve causes a large price change.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Elasticity and Indirect Taxes</h2>
    <p>An indirect tax shifts the supply curve left, raising the price consumers pay. The effect depends on PED:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>If demand is <strong>inelastic</strong>: Qd does not fall much. The tax raises significant government revenue but does little to change behaviour. Effective for raising revenue (e.g. cigarettes, petrol).</li>
      <li>If demand is <strong>elastic</strong>: Qd falls substantially. The tax changes behaviour significantly but raises less revenue. Effective for correcting externalities (e.g. plastic bags).</li>
    </ul>

    <figure class="graph-figure wide">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/tax-incidence-elasticity.svg" alt="Two panels showing the tax burden falling mostly on consumers when demand is inelastic and mostly on producers when demand is elastic" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The same tax: with inelastic demand consumers bear most of the burden; with elastic demand producers bear most.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Income Elasticity of Demand (YED)</h2>
    <p>YED measures the responsiveness of demand to a change in consumer income.</p>

    <div class="formula-box">
      <div class="formula-label">YED Formula</div>
      <div class="formula">YED = % change in Qd ÷ % change in income</div>
    </div>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>YED value</th><th>Type of good</th><th>Example</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Negative</td><td>Inferior good — demand falls as income rises</td><td>Instant noodles, second-hand goods</td></tr>
        <tr><td>0 &lt; YED &lt; 1</td><td>Normal necessity — demand rises less than proportionately with income</td><td>Basic food (rice, bread)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>YED &gt; 1</td><td>Luxury good — demand rises more than proportionately with income</td><td>Luxury handbags, overseas holidays, restaurant meals</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p style="margin-top:1rem;">The <strong>Engel curve</strong> plots quantity demanded against income for the same good — it can show different classifications at different income levels.</p>

    <div class="callout hl-only">
      <div class="callout-title">HL only — YED and the structure of economies</div>
      <p>As incomes rise across an economy, demand shifts toward luxury goods and services (high YED) and away from basic necessities and inferior goods (low or negative YED). This explains why the agricultural share of GDP falls as economies develop, while services grow. Firms can use YED to identify which sectors will grow fastest as incomes rise.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Sector</th><th>Typical YED</th><th>As incomes rise</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Primary (agriculture, raw materials)</td><td>Low</td><td>Share of the economy shrinks</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Manufacturing</td><td>Above 1</td><td>Grows faster than income, then its share shrinks after a point</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Services</td><td>Often high</td><td>Fastest growth; share expands</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>That's why economies tend to move from agriculture to manufacturing to services.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

<div class="tab-content" id="worked-2-5">
  <h2>Worked Example — Calculating PED</h2>
  <p>The price of sneakers rises from $100 to $120. Quantity demanded falls from 500 to 400.</p>

  <h3>Step 1: Calculate the changes</h3>
  <div class="answer-content" style="display:block;">
    ΔQ = 400 − 500 = −100 &nbsp;&nbsp; Q = 500<br>
    ΔP = 120 − 100 = +20 &nbsp;&nbsp; P = 100
  </div>

  <h3>Step 2: Apply the formula</h3>
  <div class="answer-content" style="display:block;">
    PED = (ΔQ/Q) ÷ (ΔP/P) = (−100/500) ÷ (20/100) = −0.2 ÷ 0.2 = <strong>−1</strong>
  </div>

  <h3>Step 3: Interpret</h3>
  <div class="answer-content" style="display:block;">
    |PED| = 1 → Unit elastic. A 20% rise in price leads to exactly a 20% fall in quantity demanded. Total revenue is unchanged.
  </div>

  <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Worked Example — Calculating YED</h2>
  <p>Original income: $3,000. New income: $3,600. Original Qd of luxury handbags: 2/year. New Qd: 4/year.</p>

  <div class="answer-content" style="display:block;">
    % change in Qd = (4 − 2)/2 × 100 = +100%<br>
    % change in income = (3,600 − 3,000)/3,000 × 100 = +20%<br>
    YED = 100 ÷ 20 = <strong>+5</strong><br>
    YED &gt; 1 → luxury good. Demand is highly income elastic.
  </div>
</div>

<div class="tab-content" id="practice-2-5">

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">Warm-up: your elasticities</div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">The price of each of these rises 50%. Would you cut back a lot, a little, or not at all? (a) Your favourite snack (b) Petrol for your family's car or bike (c) Fees at your favourite university (d) Fees at every university worldwide. What explains the difference between (c) and (d)?</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          (c) is more elastic than (d): one university has close substitutes (other universities), but "all universities" has almost none. That's the <strong>width of definition</strong> determinant.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">PED: one step</div>
    <p>Then sketch each as part of a demand curve.</p>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">1. Chocolate bars: price up 20%, quantity bought down 10%. Calculate PED, classify it, and say what happens to revenue.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          PED = −10 ÷ 20 = <strong>−0.5</strong>. Inelastic, so revenue rises.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">2. Petrol: price up 15%, quantity demanded down 5%.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          PED = −5 ÷ 15 = <strong>−0.33</strong>. Inelastic, so revenue rises.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">3. Movie tickets: price up 25%, ticket sales down 40%.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          PED = −40 ÷ 25 = <strong>−1.6</strong>. Elastic, so revenue falls.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">4. Salt: price up 50%, quantity bought down 1%.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          PED = −1 ÷ 50 = <strong>−0.02</strong>. Highly inelastic, so revenue rises.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">PED: two steps</div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">1. Headphones: price rises from $100 to $120, sales fall from 1,000 to 800.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          %ΔQ = −200/1,000 = −20%. %ΔP = 20/100 = +20%. PED = <strong>−1</strong>, unit elastic. (Revenue: $100,000 to $96,000. It isn't exactly unchanged because the % changes are measured from the starting values.)
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">2. Latte: price rises from $4 to $5, daily sales fall from 200 to 140.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          %ΔQ = −60/200 = −30%. %ΔP = 1/4 = +25%. PED = <strong>−1.2</strong>, elastic. Revenue falls from $800 to $700.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">3. Concert: price falls from $80 to $60, attendance rises from 10,000 to 15,000.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          %ΔQ = +50%. %ΔP = −25%. PED = <strong>−2</strong>, elastic. Revenue rises from $800,000 to $900,000.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">4. Gym: membership rises from $50 to $55 a month, members fall from 500 to 475.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          %ΔQ = −25/500 = −5%. %ΔP = 5/50 = +10%. PED = <strong>−0.5</strong>, inelastic. Revenue rises from $25,000 to $26,125.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">Stretch: questions in every direction</div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">1. Price up 10%, PED 0.5. Large or small % change in quantity demanded?</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          Small: about a 5% fall.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">2. PED is 2. The firm wants quantity demanded up 10%. What % price change?</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          About a 5% price cut.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">3. Price cut 20%, PED 1.5. Expected % rise in quantity demanded?</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          About 30%.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">4. Price up 10%, quantity demanded down 15%. PED?</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          1.5, so demand is price elastic.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">5. A business sells 1,000 units at $20. Price falls 10%, quantity demanded rises 20%. What happens to total revenue?</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          Rises from $20,000 to $21,600 ($18 × 1,200).
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">6. A business sells 500 units at $40. Price rises 10%, quantity demanded falls 5%. What happens to total revenue?</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          Rises from $20,000 to $20,900 ($44 × 475).
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">7. A cinema has lots of empty seats and price elastic demand. Raise or cut prices to increase revenue?</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          Cut. The % rise in quantity demanded is bigger than the % price cut, so revenue rises.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">8. A product has price inelastic demand. If the firm raises its price, will total revenue rise or fall?</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          Rise. Quantity demanded falls proportionately less than the price rises.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">Slay Kids concert: where should tickets be priced?</div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">The organiser wants to maximise revenue. Complete the PED (from each price to the next price up) and total revenue columns.
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Price (VND)</th><th>Quantity demanded</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1,800,000</td><td>30,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2,800,000</td><td>22,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3,800,000</td><td>18,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5,000,000</td><td>12,315</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6,500,000</td><td>5,500</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Price (VND)</th><th>PED to next price</th><th>Total revenue (VND)</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>1,800,000</td><td>−26.7% ÷ 55.6% = −0.48</td><td>54.0 billion</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2,800,000</td><td>−18.2% ÷ 35.7% = −0.51</td><td>61.6 billion</td></tr>
              <tr><td>3,800,000</td><td>−31.6% ÷ 31.6% = −1.0</td><td><strong>68.4 billion</strong></td></tr>
              <tr><td>5,000,000</td><td>−55.3% ÷ 30.0% = −1.84</td><td>61.6 billion</td></tr>
              <tr><td>6,500,000</td><td></td><td>35.75 billion</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          Price at <strong>3,800,000 VND</strong>. Below it, demand is inelastic, so raising the price raises revenue. Above it, demand turns elastic, so raising the price loses revenue.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout remember">
    <div class="callout-title">Note</div>
    <p>The worked answers to the concert table and the curve-matching tasks below are pending teacher review.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">Steep or flat?</div>
    <p>Ask: how willing and able are people to change consumption? Can they cut back? Are there substitutes? How much does the price rise hurt?</p>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">Which demand curve, steep (inelastic) or flat (elastic), best fits: cigarettes; petrol in the short term; petrol in the long term; pens; luxury pens; food; McDonald's; coffee as a whole; Starbucks?</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          Steep: cigarettes (addictive), petrol short term (few substitutes yet), pens (tiny share of income), food (necessity), coffee as a whole (broad definition). Flat: petrol long term (time to switch), luxury pens (luxury), McDonald's and Starbucks (narrow definition, many substitutes).
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">For each pair, which is more elastic? Coke vs iPhone; food vs Choco Pies; a foreign holiday vs a Snickers bar; one year to adjust vs one day.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          iPhone (big share of income); Choco Pies (narrow definition, luxury); the foreign holiday (luxury, big share of income); one year (more time to adjust).
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">Indirect taxes: revenue or behaviour?</div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">Which taxes would mainly raise revenue, and which would mainly change behaviour? Cigarettes, plastic bags, alcohol, luxury cars, petrol, sugary drinks, concert tickets, electricity, fast fashion, mobile phone data.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Raise revenue (inelastic demand):</strong> petrol, cigarettes, alcohol, electricity, mobile data. People keep buying.<br><strong>Change behaviour (elastic demand):</strong> plastic bags, sugary drinks, luxury cars, concert tickets, fast fashion. Easy substitutes, or the purchase can be delayed or skipped.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">YED: calculate and classify</div>
    <p>Do as many as you need to.</p>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">1. Cinema tickets: income $2,000 to $2,200; 4 to 6 tickets a month.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          %ΔQd = +50%, %ΔY = +10%. YED = <strong>+5.0</strong>, luxury.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">2. Rice: income $1,500 to $1,650; 20 kg to 19 kg a month.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          −5% ÷ +10% = <strong>−0.5</strong>, inferior.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">3. Luxury handbags: income $3,000 to $3,600; 2 to 4 bags a year.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          +100% ÷ +20% = <strong>+5.0</strong>, luxury.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">4. Public bus rides: income $1,200 to $1,500; 30 to 20 rides a month.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          −33.3% ÷ +25% = <strong>−1.33</strong>, inferior.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">5. Organic fruit: income $2,400 to $2,800; 10 kg to 14 kg a month.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          +40% ÷ +16.7% = <strong>+2.4</strong>, luxury.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">6. Instant noodles: income $900 to $1,200; 40 to 25 packs a month.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          −37.5% ÷ +33.3% = <strong>−1.13</strong>, inferior.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">7. Concert tickets: income $2,500 to $3,000; 3 to 5 tickets a year.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          +66.7% ÷ +20% = <strong>+3.3</strong>, luxury.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">8. Bottled water: income $1,800 to $2,100; 15 to 16 bottles a week.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          +6.7% ÷ +16.7% = <strong>+0.4</strong>, normal necessity.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">Frau Keller's recession</div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">Frau Keller owns a German restaurant. Outside, a takeaway stand sells a cheap menu of bratwurst with bread rolls and potato salad. Inside, she serves a luxury menu of veal schnitzel with white asparagus and fine wine. A recession is coming. Should she switch resources to the outside menu or the inside one? Use YED.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          The outside menu. Incomes fall in a recession. The inside menu is a luxury (YED &gt; 1), so demand falls more than proportionately. The cheap menu has low or even negative YED, so demand holds up or rises as diners trade down.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

